-- title:
--   Fibre of an unramified A[X]-algebra at X is a DVR
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a discrete valuation ring which is a domain, and let $\varpi \in A$ generate its maximal ideal, i.e. $\mathfrak m_A = (\varpi)$. Let $S$ be a commutative local ring and let $\varphi \colon A[X] \to S$ be a ring homomorphism which is formally unramified (`φ.FormallyUnramified`) and essentially of finite type (`φ.EssFiniteType`) for the algebra structure it induces, and suppose $\varphi(X)$ lies in the maximal ideal of $S$. Let $T$ be a commutative domain and $\pi \colon S \to T$ a surjective ring homomorphism whose kernel is the principal ideal generated by $\varphi(\varpi)$ (the image of the constant polynomial $\varpi$), and assume $\pi(\varphi(X)) \neq 0$. Then $T$ carries the structure of a discrete valuation ring, and its maximal ideal is the principal ideal generated by $\pi(\varphi(X))$; the conclusion is packaged as the existence of an `IsDiscreteValuationRing T` instance together with the identity $\mathfrak m_T = (\pi(\varphi(X)))$.
--
--   This is the statement that the fibre over the closed point of the base of an essentially unramified, essentially finite type local algebra over $A[X]$, taken at a point where the coordinate $X$ does not vanish in the fibre, is a discrete valuation ring uniformised by the image of $X$. It is used in the analysis of the supersingular locus on affine charts of modular curves of full level, where it supplies the discrete valuation rings attached to such charts.
